# Article: Project

## Language and layout

- Language: C# / .NET 10 (solution `crap4net.slnx`)
- Production code: `src/` — four files, deliberately small: Program.cs
  (CLI + report), LcovParser.cs, ComplexityWalker.cs (Roslyn),
  CrapAnalyzer.cs. Smallness is a feature: this tool gates other
  codebases, so it must stay auditable in one sitting. Resist new
  files and abstractions unless a fix genuinely needs them.
- Tests: `tests/crap4net.Tests.csproj` (xUnit)
- This is a QUALITY GATE consumed by swarm agents in other repos that
  trust its exit codes blindly. False greens are the worst possible
  defect class; when in doubt, fail loudly.

## Commands

- Build: `dotnet build` (warnings-as-errors stays on)
- Unit tests: `dotnet test`
- Lint/format: `dotnet format`
- Self-check (dogfood): coverage via
  `dotnet test --collect:"XPlat Code Coverage;Format=lcov"`, then run
  the tool on itself: `dotnet run --project src -- --lcov <coverage.info> src`

## Quality bars

Universal — these gate EVERY acceptance and handoff:

- `dotnet build` clean, 0 warnings
- All tests green: `dotnet test`
- `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` clean
- Exit-code contract stated in `--help` matches the implementation
  exactly; every distinct exit path has a test.
- Tests must be OS-portable: no absolute POSIX paths in expectations
  (this suite must pass on Windows; one current test violates this
  and is part of the work).

## Code style

- Two-space indentation, `.editorconfig` enforced; `dotnet format`
  must stay clean.
- Tests: xUnit with bare `Assert.*` — that is THIS repo's idiom
  (unlike other bandoyer repos); match the existing file.
- XML doc comments on public types/members, matching the existing
  density.
- Commits: plain succinct subjects, no trailers, evidence in the body.

## Notes

- README.md documents the CLI surface; any flag or exit-code change
  updates it in the same commit.
- The LcovParser's duplicate-SF merge uses max-hits semantics on
  purpose (multi-suite line coverage); preserve it.
